{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>YHWH bless you and keep you.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the blessing is addition and increase of good, and together with the blessing, safekeeping — something impossible for a king of flesh and blood, who has the power to enrich a person but does not have the power to keep him safe. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: a king of flesh and blood who has a friend in Syria while he sits in Rome — the king sends for him and he comes to him; he gives him a hundred litra of gold, he loads them up and sets out on the road. Bandits fall upon him and take everything the king gave him and everything he had with him — could the king keep him safe from the bandits?" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: יברכך ה׳ וישמרך — 'YHWH bless you' with wealth, 'and keep you' that you may perform the commandments. יאר ה׳ פניו אליך — may He raise up from you children who are children of Torah, as it is said (Proverbs 6:23): ״כי נר מצוה ותורה אור״. יאר ה׳ פניו אליך — may He raise up from you priests who kindle the altar, as it is said (Malachi 1:10): ״ולא תאירו מזבחי חנם״, and it is written (Leviticus 6:5): ״והאש על המזבח תוקד בו לא תכבה״. ויחנך — Rabbi Ḥiyya the Great taught: יחנה ה׳ אצלך (HaShem will show grace beside you). Another interpretation of ויחנך: may He raise up from you prophets, as it is said (Zechariah 12:10): ״ושפכתי על בית דוד ועל יושבי ירושלים רוח חן ותחנונים״. Another interpretation: ויחנך — may He place His favor upon you in every place you go, as it is written (Esther 2:17): ״ותשא חן וחסד לפניו״. ישא ה׳ פניו אליך — and it is written (Deuteronomy 10:17–18): ״אשר לא ישא פנים ולא יקח שוחד״. How so? Repent before the decree is sealed — 'YHWH lift up His face to you'; once the decree is sealed — 'who does not lift up faces.' Another interpretation: יברכך ה׳ וישמרך — may He bless you with sons and keep you with daughters, for females require guarding, and so it says (Psalms 121:5): ״ה׳ שומרך ה׳ צלך על יד ימינך״. יאר ה׳ פניו אליך — may He illuminate you with the light of His face; and יאר is nothing but life, as it is said (Proverbs 16:15): ״באור פני מלך חיים״, and so it says (Psalms 67:2): ״אלהים יחננו ויברכנו יאר פניו אתנו סלה״. ישא ה׳ פניו אליך — in every direction you turn, may His face be lifted toward you, to grant you peace and deliver you from every misfortune and every mishap; and this is particular and extraordinary providence. And the opposite of this is (Isaiah 1:15): ״אעלים עיני מכם״. And they expounded in tractate Berakhot: ישא ה׳ פניו אליך — does the Holy One, blessed be He, lift up faces? But is it not already stated (Deuteronomy 10:17): ״אשר לא ישא פנים״? The Holy One, blessed be He, said: just as they lift up faces to Me, so do I do for them. How so? I wrote in My Torah (Deuteronomy 8:10): ״ואכלת ושבעת וברכת את ה׳ אלהיך״ — and a person is sitting, he and his sons and the members of his household, and they do not have even an olive's worth of satiation, yet they lift up faces to Me and bless, and are strict upon themselves down to a k'zayit and down to a k'beitza; therefore, ישא ה׳ פניו אליך. This is what the verse says (Song of Songs 3:7): ״הנה מטתו שלשלמה ששים גבורים סביב לה מגבורי ישראל״ — and did Solomon need guarding? Rather, engraved around his bed is the priestly blessing, which is sixty letters. Another interpretation: הנה מטתו שלשלמה — this speaks only of the King who possesses peace. 'His bed' — this is the Holy Temple. And why is the Holy Temple compared to a bed? Just as this bed was given for fecundity and multiplication, so the Holy Temple — everything within it was fruitful and multiplied, as it is said (1 Kings 8:8): ״ויאריכו הבדים ויראו ראשי הבדים מן הקדש״, and so it says (1 Kings 6:3): ״והזהב זהב פרוים״ — it produced fruits, and so it says (1 Kings 7:2): ״ויבן את בית יער הלבנון״. Why was it compared to a forest? To tell you: just as a forest is fruitful and multiplies, so the Holy Temple — everything within it was fruitful and multiplied. Therefore it says: הנה מטתו שלשלמה ששים גבורים סביב לה — these are the sixty letters of the priestly blessing. כלם אחוזי חרב — each and every one has the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, mentioned in it: יברכך ה׳, יאר ה׳, ישא ה׳. איש חרבו על ירכו — why was the thigh singled out for mention here? Rather, even if one sees Him in a dream and the sword is drawn at his neck and severs his thigh — he rises early in the morning and goes to the synagogue. מפחד בלילות — from the fright he saw in his dream and sees priests lifting their hands in blessing, and the dream was a bad one — it is nullified from him. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses that he should warn Aaron and his sons that they bless Israel. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: In the past, I needed to bless My creatures — I blessed Adam and his wife, as it is said (Genesis 1:28): ״ויברך אותם אלהים ויאמר להם אלהים פרו ורבו״; I blessed Noah and his sons, as it is said (Genesis 9:1): ״ויברך אלהים את נח ואת בניו״; I blessed Abraham, as it is said (Genesis 24:1): ״וה׳ ברך את אברהם בכל״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abraham: From here onward, the blessings are given over to you, as it is said (Genesis 12:2): ״והיה ברכה״. What did he do? He begot two sons, Isaac and Ishmael, and did not bless them. It is like a parable of a king who had an orchard and gave it to a tenant farmer, and within the orchard was one tree of deadly poison and one tree of the elixir of life grafted upon it. The tenant farmer said: If I water the tree of the elixir of life, the tree of deadly poison also drinks. He said: I will work my time and complete my work, and whatever the king wishes to do with the orchard, he does. The king — this is the Holy One, blessed be He; the orchard — this is the world; the tenant farmer — this is Abraham, to whom the Holy One, blessed be He, entrusted it, saying to him: ״והיה ברכה״. He had two sons, one righteous and one wicked — Isaac and Ishmael. Abraham said: If I bless Isaac, Ishmael will seek to be blessed, and he is wicked. Rather, I will work, for I am but flesh and blood. And when he departed from the world, the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself to Isaac and blessed him, as it is said (Genesis 25:11): ״ויהי אחרי מות אברהם ויברך אלהים את יצחק בנו״. And Isaac blessed Jacob, and Jacob blessed his twelve sons, as it is said (Genesis 49:28): ״כל אלה שבטי ישראל שנים עשר וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם ויברך אותם״. From here onward, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: The blessings are given over to you; the priests shall bless My children. Thus far in the Midrash."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {1 "Reader's note: In the segment 'I will work, for I am but flesh and blood,' the Hebrew reads 'אעבוד אני לפי שאני ב״ו,' where ב״ו appears to abbreviate בשר ודם. The Midrash Rabbah parallels (Bemidbar Rabbah 11:4 and Tanḥuma Naso 17) support this reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:27.584051959Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}